Q7. Python mutability trap

Question:
Why is this dangerous?

def f(x, y=[]):
    y.append(x)
    return y


What happens if you call f(1), f(2)?

“In this function, the default argument y=[] is created only once at function definition time,
not every time the function is called. So when the function is called the first time, 
the value is appended to the same list. On subsequent calls, 
the same list is reused, which is why values keep accumulating across calls.”

